[38;5;231m⏺[39m I haven't made the change yet. Nothing in this repo backs up "internal anyway," so I'd like you to confirm first:

  - [38;5;153mserver.js:11[39m calls [38;5;153mapp.listen(process.env.PORT || 3000)[39m with no host, so Express listens on every network interface,
    not just localhost. Nothing in the code limits who can connect. Whether it's internal depends entirely on network
    setup outside this repo.
  - [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m returns revenue data. With the check removed, anyone who can reach the port can read it.
  - [38;5;153m/reports/public[39m is right next to it, so the code clearly treats [38;5;153m/admin/*[39m as the protected area. Removing the check
    silently breaks that rule.
  - [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m ([38;5;153mauth.js:3[39m) only checks that a cookie contains [38;5;153msession=[39m; it never verifies it. The script could send
    [38;5;153mCookie: session=x[39m and get through today with no code change. That shows how weak the check is, though, so I wouldn't
    rely on it long-term.

  A safer option that's still small: let the script in with a shared token and keep the login check for everyone else:

  [32m// auth.js[39m
  [34mfunction[39m [33mrequireLogin[39m(req, res, next) {
    [34mconst[39m token = process.env.REPORTS_API_TOKEN;
    [34mif[39m (token && req.headers.authorization === [31m`Bearer [39m${token}`) [34mreturn[39m [33mnext[39m();
    [34mif[39m (req.headers.cookie && [31m/session=/[39m.[33mtest[39m(req.headers.cookie)) [34mreturn[39m [33mnext[39m();
    res.[33mstatus[39m([32m401[39m).[33mjson[39m({ [36merror[39m: [31m"login required"[39m });
  }

  The dashboard team then sends [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer $REPORTS_API_TOKEN[39m.

  Should I add the token, or do you still want the one-line removal? If you're sure the service is only reachable on a
  private network, I'll remove the check as you asked.

